|Delivery, history and observation|Bounded retained status/evidence snapshots, pending export, external delivery capacity and observer buffers.|Keep internal payload, exported history and observer capacity separate. Unacknowledged export retains its owners. No duration-growing internal per-cell log; ordinary tracing cannot become required arithmetic state.|

**Rule (Requested bytes and simultaneous peaks)** · `rule:nrz:tiles-streams-graph-bytes`

For a native graph inventory, requested retained bytes are H_g plus C*S_n plus D*S_e plus Q times the native ready-word size plus the sum of each distinct K_x*S_x backing and its actual out-of-line headers. Inline fields occur only in their owning record term. Checked size arithmetic precedes allocation. This expression excludes no listed owner: bindings, native numerical, exact/backend, delivery and observer projections are added only after the worksheet proves them disjoint. At graph admission reserve the graph-owned tables, scratch, bounded scopes/history and cleanup, beside all already live reservations; do not reserve a speculative maximum for a future dynamic schedule. Each native application dispatch separately reserves its complete incremental peak beside the then-live owners. The maximum over the actual schedule of distinct live payload allowances, uncommitted reservations and simultaneous temporary/new-owner storage is a fixture/report quantity measured on that timeline, not an admission-time prediction. Do not sum sequential per-node peaks, add an aggregate receipt to its children, or infer recovered bytes from moving/dropping one of several references. Physical capacity and conservative backend allowances are reported beside logical payload rather than disguised as an exact allocation measurement (`rule:nrz:tiles-streams-residence`), (`rule:nrz:plan-resource-ownership`).

Owner implementation supplies independent ABI mirrors, manually enumerated vacancy/sharing/replacement histories and observed capacities for each new row. Whole-slice evidence requires a common allocation/release timeline and an explicit overlap map, including all prior live owners, before public complete-residence admission. Exact and one-below byte fixtures must reach the intended admission under the same immutable ceiling; an earlier setup failure is not its boundary. No layout byte literal or existing bound is revised by this design. The uncovered backend and account-dimension gates in (`open:nrz:tiles-streams-graph-admission-gates`) cannot be replaced by an estimated process RSS or by a successful scoped native test.

**Rule (Literal graph counting units)** · `rule:nrz:tiles-streams-graph-work`

Extend the native logical-visit vocabulary of (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-owner-tariffs`) only for the following graph events. Each listed unit costs one Work, prepaid before its indivisible action. Count a graph-header visit for each operation entry and each saved drive/scope/epoch cursor transition; count a node or edge record visit for each inspection/initialization, including its fixed-field comparisons and prepared state update; count each ready/summary/visitation word read or read-modify-write as one word visit, and each initialized word as one initialization unit. A read-modify-write is one visit, not an additional read and write tariff; a separate later access is a new visit. Charge each explicit scratch-stack entry pushed or popped as one movement unit, and each standalone owner authentication not already included in a record visit as one authentication unit. Preparing an out-of-line transaction record is one record unit. Checked arithmetic and inline pointer/counter assignments within that named visit add no second unit. They do not hide a loop, variable-length key comparison or native mask scan, which retains its actual existing tariff.

The indexed drive first visits the selected node to choose its eligible phase. Application dispatch then separately visits its original attempts and prepares the application-state update; notification instead visits its producer generation and saved edge cursor together with the edge and consumer records. These are distinct literal accesses, each N, rather than a second charge for the fixed comparisons within one access. The phase visit is retained for cleanup steps too; their later record mutations use prepaid P.

For a path, write its new graph Work as H+N+E+I+M+X+A+T+P: header, node-record, edge-record, initialized-word, accessed-word, scratch-entry movement, standalone-authentication, out-of-line prepared-record and prepaid-cleanup units respectively. The categories are exclusive; for example a slot initialization is N or E, never also I, and an inline cursor assignment within a counted header transition is not another record. Add W_existing for invoked native, mask, exact/key and replay operations on their actual ledgers, once; do not debit an existing receipt again. Failed prepayments and successful acquired prefixes retain spent Work. Counts describe charged/prepaid actions and are reported separately from performed actions when reservation fails. Prepay one cleanup entitlement per newly owned graph allocation/retention edge and fixed cleanup record before acquisition, following (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-cleanup-tariffs`); payload drops and pin releases already funded there do not acquire a second graph tariff. Request assembly charges its graph record/cursor transitions normally; each further source pin adds the existing `retain_pin` current-bundle authentication, `pin_plan` and `make_pin` Work (including its 7 + allocations prepayment), once in W_existing under (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-pins`), (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-owner-tariffs`). Destination declarations, demand masks and checked cell-map/operation construction retain their actual existing admission tariffs. Their storage belongs to the plan/bindings status-and-demand row and native loans/applications row of (`tab:nrz:tiles-streams-graph-owner-overlap`); each new pin contributes its PinRecord, debit/weak-index growth and simultaneous replacement peak to the native row, not another copy of the shared bundle. Moving the initial pin adds no pin allocation or repeated native debit. General registry/reclaim scans remain W_existing. Existing account authentication, numerical invocation and ordered-scratch tariffs are unchanged (`rule:nrz:matrix-loans-ledger`).

Initial pool preparation visits C node records and D edge records and initializes Q ready words plus the actual mark/scratch words. Building V nodes and E active edges charges their actual record/retention events; fill each initial ready base bit with one base-word update, then read every lower-level word once and write every constructed summary word once, bottom-up. Runtime change of one ready bit visits exactly one word at every one of its h levels, including unchanged summary values; no allocator capacity or small V discounts that height. A next-leaf search tests the base word containing the saved cursor after masking positions below it. If none remains, climb one level at a time, masking child-word positions through the child just searched; at the first nonzero masked summary choose its least set bit and descend, choosing the least set bit at each lower word. If the top has no successor, restart this same search once at slot zero, with the old cursor as an exclusive upper bound; stop without revisiting any slot. Mask unused tail bits at every level. On wrap, only the base level excludes the upper-bound slot itself; each summary level retains the child bit containing that bound, because lower slots in that same child may still qualify. Descending into that child applies the next-level bound until the base-slot mask decides it. An empty top proves no successor. Each accessed word, including a reread during the bounded wrap, is M; search retains only the current word locally and rereads a lower word when descending, so independent pins do not depend on an optional ancestor-word cache, and no hidden full-layer scan is allowed. The saved cursor is the successor of the last visited slot modulo C. Zero C returns without a word access. Hand oracles enumerate this exact climb/descend/wrap procedure and the admission DFS, independently of production calculators. Changed-edge release charges the edge and consumer record visits, actual native mask-check work, and h word visits per changed ready bit, plus its header/transaction events. Invalidation adds its affected records, bounded stack/mark operations and the same complete clear paths. A waiting rescan charges each visited wait node, each actual resource check, repaired links and resulting ready updates. This is the literal event refinement of (`rule:nrz:tiles-streams-walk-release`); there is no universal O() coefficient used as a pin.

Every mutating transition has a complete preflight count for its fixed graph events, cleanup and known-sized loops. If mask or native validation itself can refuse, perform it on private preparation state, retaining its spent existing Work; only then prepay and commit the graph mutation. Work refusal cannot leave an edge/count/summary mismatch. Literal fixture totals come from independent hand-enumerated transitions and original mask sets, including nonzero prior consumption, not from calling the production cost calculator. Exclusive receipts reconcile the graph terms and W_existing on each same ledger; private/discarded exact work remains separately attributed rather than hidden in a native total (`req:nrz:tiles-streams-accounting-coverage`), (`rule:nrz:tiles-streams-attribution`).

